<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Deliberately minimal accessibility service configuration.

    accessibilityEventTypes="typeViewTextChanged|typeViewLongClicked|typeViewSelected"
        The ONLY three event types this service subscribes to.

        typeViewTextChanged powers the typed "?en"/"?pl" trigger pathway:
        "the text in the field currently being edited changed."

        typeViewLongClicked powers the separate long-press "translate what's
        under my finger" bubble pathway, added later: "the user long-pressed
        a view." This is the standard, purpose-built accessibility signal
        for a long-press and requires no additional capability flag to
        receive — in particular it does NOT require
        flagRequestTouchExplorationMode, which would turn the whole device
        into TalkBack-style screen-reader navigation and was deliberately
        rejected as a way to detect this gesture. One direct consequence of
        using this event instead of raw touch tracking: this service cannot
        observe exactly when the user's finger lifts off the screen, only
        that a long-press happened — which is why the resulting translation
        bubble (see TranslationBubble.kt) dismisses itself via an explicit
        close button, a tap outside the bubble, or an auto-dismiss timer,
        rather than "on finger release."

        typeViewSelected was added after real-device testing showed some
        apps' message lists (confirmed: the stock/Google Messages SMS app)
        never dispatch typeViewLongClicked at all for a long-press —
        unlike WhatsApp and Telegram, which do. The most likely reason is
        that such apps implement "long-press to enter multi-select mode"
        via their own touch/gesture handling rather than
        View.performLongClick(), the standard path that dispatches
        typeViewLongClicked. Entering that selection state, however, is
        expected to still mark the selected row as isSelected — a state
        change the platform generally expects apps to announce to
        accessibility services (for screen readers), which is what
        typeViewSelected reports. This is still a standard,
        purpose-built accessibility signal — not raw touch tracking — and
        still requires no additional capability flag.

        This service still does NOT receive typeViewFocused,
        typeWindowStateChanged, typeWindowContentChanged, typeViewClicked
        (a normal, non-long tap), or any other event type — it still never
        observes navigation, scrolling, or which screens the user visits,
        only "a field's text changed," "a view was long-pressed," and "a
        view became selected."

    We deliberately do NOT set:
      - android:flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows — this service never enumerates
        or inspects any window other than the one that raised the event.
      - android:flagIncludeNotImportantViews — decorative/non-input views are
        never read.
      - android:packageNames — left unset (all packages) at the OS
        subscription level ONLY because the OS accessibility API has no
        concept of a *dynamically user-editable* allow-list here; the real
        enforcement happens first thing in
        TextGateAccessibilityService.onAccessibilityEvent(), which checks
        event.packageName against the user's saved allow-list and the
        built-in block-list and returns immediately for anything not
        explicitly allowed, before any further processing. This is true for
        ALL THREE event types: the long-press bubble pathway (long-click AND
        selected) is gated by BubbleTranslateGate, which enforces the exact
        same block-list and allow-list as the typed-trigger pathway (see
        BubbleTranslateGate.kt).

    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true" is required for
    AccessibilityEvent#getSource() to return a usable node at all; without
    it the service could not read the field being edited, nor the
    long-pressed/selected message content, even after the user grants
    permission. This is intentionally the ONLY window-content capability
    granted — the service never calls AccessibilityService#getWindows() or
    #getRootInActiveWindow(), and never enumerates any window other than
    the one that raised the event; it only ever inspects event.getSource()
    and — for the long-press bubble pathway ONLY, when that node's own
    text is blank — that SAME node's own descendants (never siblings,
    never ancestors, bounded search; see BubbleTranslateGate.kt's
    "Where the text actually lives" doc section for the full reasoning).
    The typed-trigger pathway (EventGate) still only ever reads
    event.getSource() directly, with no further traversal of any kind.

    android:notificationTimeout="0" — events are delivered immediately, not
    batched, so no accessibility event is ever buffered/queued by the
    framework on our behalf.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeViewTextChanged|typeViewLongClicked|typeViewSelected"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags=""
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:notificationTimeout="0"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:settingsActivity="com.textgate.ai.settings.SettingsActivity" />
